\f1\fs22 Hayley\'92s anticipated her second encounter with Stasis Anomalies Investigator Chip Stockwell would be much more fun. Eloise\'92s lawyers had contacted Fridge Force to negotiate a settlement of certain matters regarding a malfunctioning fridge on Shakti Enterprises corporate property, and since this was a matter for corporate law which, in Ulyssia, tipped the scales being held by the blind bitch called Impartiality very much in favour of corporations, said lawyers respectfully  enquired whether some civilised settlement of the matter could be agreed to before someone felt compelled to pull a trigger or push a button of some kind or another.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Fridge Force agreed to negotiations, provided Eloise attended.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise agreed to attend, provided negotiations were discretely conducted in public, and provided Stasis Anomalies Investigator Chip Stockwell attended. And was the only attendee for Fridge Force.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Fine, said Fridge Force, as long as Eloise was the sole attendee for Shakti Enterprises.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Fine, said the Shakti Enterprises lawyers. How does next Tuesday for High Tea sound? Please ensure Stasis Anomalies Investigator Chip Stockwell makes an effort with his attire.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip felt stupid in his newly made bespoke suit and tie. The brogues on his feet seemed to squeeze all the blood to his brain. Or was it from his brain? He was certainly light headed, but it could have been the sudden acceleration and quickly following deceleration of the lift, taking him to the international restaurant for stupid negotiations over tidbits of cakes and savouries that would be too small to pick up in his fingers and he would be forced to figure out which piece of cutlery he was supposed to use for which cake or pastry. He\'92d get it wrong and he\'92d be humiliated. He could simply not eat, but he was hungry. Chip Stockwell was always hungry.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>But for all Chip\'92s apprehension and misgivings, the thing he liked least was the name of the restaurant: 
\f2\i Gravity Well
\f1\i0 . He\'92d pondered on it since Eloise\'92s people had nominated the venue. Was it just a coincidence, or did someone know something they shouldn\'92t know? Perhaps it was just a joke; someone thought it would be funny to call anything you had to be catapulted 
\f2\i up
\f1\i0  to in a turbo lift, a gravity well. Chip hoped so.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The lift opened and light poured in. Chip believed he\'92d been cast into heaven, so vivid was the sky above the dome and so remote were the rooftops of any other towers in the city. Chip alighted from the lift somewhat giddily upon aching feet. He was anxious he might be sweating all the way through this feeble-fabric suit.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise waved at him from a window seat, so far away it felt like a football field of geometrically arranged white line tables had opened up between them. Chip focussed on the other seat at Eloise\'92s table, determined to reach it without passing out or tripping over his tortured feet. He looked neither left nor right, and so failed to notice a party of five seated at one of the group of humbler tables by the kitchen doors and the hall to other amenities.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Hello, Mr Stockwell,\'92 Eloise greeted without standing. \'91Please.\'92 She gestured at the empty chair.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip sat in exactly the same way a pallet of bricks falling off a truck would. He breathed a sigh of relief for his feet, and hadn\'92t realised he\'92d closed his eyes. A waiter attended the table and filled Chip\'92s water glass.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m so sorry for this,\'92 Eloise said, and Chip discovered her standing. There was no chance he could haul his bulk off the chair to mirror her. \'91I\'92m afraid I\'92ve been called away on urgent business. I\'92m sure you understand,\'92 Eloise said, and gave Chip a grandmotherly friendly smile. Behind her designer glasses, her eyes glistened with compassion and sympathy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But \'85 you agreed.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, of course,\'92 Eloise conceded and clasped her hands in front of her, rolled her shoulders and tilted her head to signify she was hearing him. \'91We could reschedule \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chips looked about in vague arcs, over the table and to the horizon outside. His head wavered. Now he was there, he couldn\'92t walk away without anything. His burning calves wouldn\'92t allow it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise, seeing Chip\'92s discomfort, chose the perfect moment to strike. \'91I can turn the meeting over to my senior executive who oversees \'85 these matters. I assure you she has my full authority. Her word is my word.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>When Chip clutched for the glass, drank deeply, and said nothing, Eloise waved someone over from behind Chip.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91May I present Hayley Lanning? I believe you have already met her in a previous capacity.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley rounded the table and took station by the seat vacated by Eloise. \'91Hello Mr Stockwell,\'92 she said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What\'92s going on?\'92 Chip found his authoritative voice, using exactly the male tone that would guarantee failure to impress Eloise, or Hayley.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Fear not, Mr Stockwell,\'92 Eloise said as she approached Chip and gave him a friendly pat on his shoulder. Then she tapped him between his shoulder blades. Chip didn\'92t like the intrusion into his personal space at all. He tried shrugging it off.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley sat at the table as Eloise leaned closer to Chip\'92s ear. \'91I\'92d appreciate you taking this meeting as planned, Mr Stockwell. I assure you Ms Lanning is acting in my full capacity for the purpose of negotiation.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91But you agreed.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise let Chip\'92s words dissipate before she spoke again. \'91I should also explain, as much as I\'92d appreciate you staying, how much I would not appreciate you leaving before we\'92ve settled matters. In fact, I\'92m so committed to achieving an outcome here, I\'92ve just applied an explosive patch between your shoulder blades.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip stiffened. \'91What?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Now please, have no concern for anyone else in the restaurant. It\'92s quite a small charge. If it should go off, none of you will even stain this fine table cloth. It will be merely sufficient to shred your heart, which will do little more than leak out over the back of you chair.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip whined. His lips trembled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You are being watched, Mr Stockwell\'97don\'92t look around. If you try to leave or make any sudden gestures towards Ms Lanning, or if Ms Lanning makes a certain gesture, rest assured this device will detonate. Am I clear?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip nodded while taking extra care that it could not be construed as a sudden gesture.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Very good,\'92 Eloise said. \'91Now, relax. It is my fervent hope you will be seeing me again very soon. May I recommend the carpaccio? I so love tissue-thin raw meat, don\'92t you? Good afternoon, Mr Stockwell.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise left. Hayley smiled. Chip felt his stomach turn to water. Would they actually kill him here in this restaurant? He cursed his stupidity for coming there at all, for walking in without checking his surroundings, for taking a seat where almost the entire space was behind his field of vision. He did, however, have a spectacular view across the Airborne city rising out of the Layer like so many asparagus tips. He hated asparagus. But he hated being complacent, which is what he\'92d  been in believing he held the cards for this meeting. Now he was frightened, when he was accustomed to being the one who frightened others. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley assessed Chip and said. \'91I\'92d like to pick up our conversation from the last time we met.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91When you were an esteemed member of the police. Now you work for an arms dealer?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Indeed. There\'92s less corruption and the pay is better.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And you\'92re prepared to murder an employee of Fridge Force?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What I\'92m prepared to do is right a wrong,\'92 Hayley said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What wrong?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I believe, last time we met, we were discussing an unfortunate family who had the misfortune to receive a visit from you.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip said nothing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The Chuglinghams?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip\'92s lips tightened.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And their even more unfortunate neighbour, Travis Teh?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip\'92s eyes narrowed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Where are the Chuglinghams, Chip?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chips jaw ground sideways as he looked the window.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91They didn\'92t sell their apartment, did they, Chip.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What is this about?\'92 Chip asked, then cursed under his breath.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable> \'91You know what I\'92m wondering, Chip? I\'92m wondering what kind of vandalism Travis Teh or his associates committed on the Chuglinghams\'92 stasis unit. It seems unlikely they just broke in as a prank, or simply to give the Chuglinghams a stinky welcome-home. I think they 
\f2\i stole 
\f1\i0 something out of the Chuglinghams stasis cupboard. Something in a kind of metal, dull-green cask, that has electrical cable attached at both ends?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip\'92s eyes bulged so much, they almost popped from the sockets.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91They normally have a ridiculous warning on them, don\'92t they? What was it again? Something like 
\f2\i Tampering with stasis technology invalidates the warranty of your life
\f1\i0 .\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The blood drained from Chip\'92s face. His feet stopped hurting. \'91
\f2\i You
\f1\i0  are the one who tampered with the stasis unit in Shakti\'92s house.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There was no such tampering with on any Shakti Enterprises property.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We have proof.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You have faulty equipment. And our lawyers can prove it. That\'92s another advantage of working for an arms dealer instead of a police force. Better lawyers. And do you know what my better lawyers have found out, Chip?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chips\'92s stare was all the answer Hayley would get.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You see, chip. My lawyers have researched every case that Fridge Force has brought to court regarding violations of the 
\f2\i Refrigeration Secrets Act
\f1\i0 . They were very thorough. It turns out there\'92s never been such a case in the history of Ulyssia.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That can\'92t be right,\'92 Chip said before he could stop himself.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It is right. Chip. It is right. Because Fridge Force never needs to bring cases before courts. Because they have tacit authority to act summarily. Fridge Force is a bully, Chip. And you know how to deal with bullies?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip exhaled. \'91You call their bluff.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That\'92s right, Chip. But you made the mistake of taking on Shakti Enterprises, and we\'92re going to do more that call a bluff. We\'92re going to make sure you can never do something like this to anyone like the Chuglinghams ever again.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip snorted. \'92Since when does an arms dealer care about collateral damage?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We\'92re branching out.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip scoffed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley paused, then asked, \'91What\'92s a Gyges Disc?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip looked genuinely terrified.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s the secret free lunch in stasis tech, isn\'92t it? The part that makes food preservation just perfect. What could possibly go wrong?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Nothing, if no one tampers with the things,\'92 Chip said defiantly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley smiled. \'91What do they do?\'92 she asked, and when Chip declined to answer, she continued. Her hubris was tasting as good as fine Antarctic champagne. \'91It\'92s in the name of the Act and in your title. Part of keeping 
\f2\i Refrigeration Secrets
\f1\i0  safe requires Stasis Anomalies Investigators like you, Chip.\'92 Hayley poured herself a glass of water and sipped. \'91What kinds of things do you investigate? Crazy people with memory loss? Perhaps they claim they can time travel? Or, perhaps a man like you takes cases of a more practical, physical nature. Perhaps ground car accidents? Little earthquakes?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip snarled. \'91You and Shakti and the whole of Shakti Enterprises are in so much trouble. You have no idea how this will escalate.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91One of the benefits of joining an arms dealer is we can war-game the consequences, Chip. You really want to take us on?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It will be a pleasure,\'92 Chip said, and almost stood up from the anger coursing through his body. But Hayley waved him back with a wagging finger. \

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Oh, right. The explosive device in his chest.
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Look, Chip. Eloise doesn\'92t want to shut you down. She only wants you to stop bullying little people like the Chuglinghams, and start negotiating with the bigger folks in town.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Negotiate how?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Eloise proposes to do research into stasis tech.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91She wants to weaponise them.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No, she wants to find other, peaceful applications, and prevent others from weaponising them.\'92 Hayley had no confidence this was true, but hoped Sarah might make it true. This was a dangerous game to play, but Fridge Force and the government were already playing it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No one will believe that.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No one has to know. Just us. And you.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And why would would we agree to that.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91To stop us from revealing certain information\'97not related to the tech itself\'97just about they way you bully people on behalf of the government, keeping them cowed and afraid, so the government can move with impunity on matters of security.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There\'92s an overgrowing population of so-called terrorists committed to secret off-shore detention. Detained without rights or any hope of freedom. All in the name of the 
\f2\i Refrigeration Secrets Act
\f1\i0 . The population is terrified of terrorists, almost as much as they are of their Christmas seafood going bad. And you have both fears covered off with the 
\f2\i Refrigeration Secrets Act
\f1\i0 . Bravo, Chip. Bravo. But unless you leave Shakti Enterprises, and all its agents, employees, successors \'85\'92 Hayley waved her hand over in a circle. \'91\'85 alone, we will expose you. Chip, we know where the Chuglinghams are.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91No, you don\'92t.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes we do. They\'92re just over there, at that table by the kitchen door, enjoying the best degustation of their past and future lives.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Despite the threat of a heart-shredding explosion, Chip whirled around and looked in the direction of Hayley\'92s gaze. To his horror, he saw the Chuglinghams and their children enjoying a meal up to their elbows.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91To be fair, I doubt they ever knew what a degustation is. They bitterly complained about the sizes of the meals, and no explanation mollified the children about the number of plates yet to come. So I persuaded the waiting staff to bring out everything at once.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Mr Chuglingham waved at Chip and she chewed a morsel in clear delight. Chip waved feebly back.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We have a full expos\'e9 planned. The campaign will be swift and devastating. You know Eloise has deep pockets, and she\'92ll win over the population.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The Airborne will never believe it.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Perhaps they won\'92t. But they will never miss the opportunity to feast on the carcass of a government or one of their most secretive agencies. To be blunt, we could tell the most outrageous lies about you, if we spend enough money on perpetuating them. But we don\'92t have to tell lies, do we, Chip. We just need the Chuglinghams. And hundreds of families just like them.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip collapsed all his weight on the table. \'91Go ahead. Just detonate my heart right now,\'92 he said, miserable.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Oh, we\'92d never actually blow your big manly heart to bits, Chip.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley\'92s statement hung between them until Chip said, \'91Okay. What else do you want?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Contracts have already been sent to Fridge Force lawyers. I must say, I was was impressed with the way they acquired your contract authentication codes.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And what is it I am meant to have signed?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Your resignation from Fridge Force. Chip Stockwell, I\'92d like to extend you employment with Shakti Enterprises.\'92
